index 5eda7d9..eb7eddc 100644
--- a/docs/topics/downloader-middleware.rst
+++ b/docs/topics/downloader-middleware.rst
@@ -225,6 +225,96 @@ middleware, see the :ref:`downloader middleware usage guide
 For a list of the components enabled by default (and their orders) see the
 :setting:`DOWNLOADER_MIDDLEWARES_BASE` setting.
 
+.. _topics-dlmw-conditional-requests:
+
+ConditionalRequestsMiddleware
+-----------------------------
+
+.. module:: scrapy.downloadermiddlewares.conditional
+   :synopsis: Conditional requests downloader middleware
+
+.. class:: ConditionalRequestsMiddleware
+
+    This middleware implements lightweight incremental re-crawling through
+    `HTTP conditional requests`_.
+
+    .. _HTTP conditional requests: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#field.if-none-match
+
+    It is part of the default downloader middleware chain, but it stays
+    disabled unless :setting:`CONDITIONAL_REQUESTS_ENABLED` is set to
+    ``True``.
+
+    A minimal configuration for a daily incremental job looks like this:
+
+    .. code-block:: python
+
+        # settings.py
+        CONDITIONAL_REQUESTS_ENABLED = True
+        CONDITIONAL_REQUESTS_DIR = "/var/cache/myproject/conditional"
+
+    With this configuration the first crawl of the day behaves as usual,
+    except that the ``ETag`` and ``Last-Modified`` values of every
+    successful response are written to
+    ``/var/cache/myproject/conditional/<spider name>_conditional_requests``.
+    Every later crawl reuses those values: unchanged pages answer ``304 Not
+    Modified`` and are silently dropped, while changed pages are parsed and
+    their new validators replace the old ones.
+
+    Unlike :class:`~scrapy.downloadermiddlewares.httpcache.HttpCacheMiddleware`,
+    this middleware does not store response bodies. It only remembers the
+    ``ETag`` and ``Last-Modified`` header values of seen responses and uses
+    them to make later requests conditional:
+
+    *   When a ``GET`` or ``HEAD`` request is about to be downloaded and a
+        previous crawl stored validators for the same request fingerprint,
+        the middleware adds ``If-None-Match`` and/or ``If-Modified-Since``
+        headers to the request. Headers that are already set on the request,
+        e.g. by the spider, are never overwritten.
+
+    *   When the origin server answers such a request with ``304 Not
+        Modified``, the response is dropped by raising
+        :exc:`~scrapy.exceptions.IgnoreRequest`, so unchanged pages never
+        reach the spider callbacks and produce no duplicate items or
+        follow-up requests.
+
+    *   When a ``200 OK`` response carries an ``ETag`` and/or a
+        ``Last-Modified`` header, those values are stored under the request
+        fingerprint, replacing any previously stored values. Responses whose
+        ``Cache-Control`` header contains ``no-store`` are not recorded.
+
+    A typical use case is a spider that runs periodically over the same
+    start URLs. With conditional requests enabled, pages that did not change
+    since the previous run cost a single round trip and are filtered out
+    automatically, while changed pages are processed normally and update the
+    stored validators.
+
+    By default validators are kept in memory, which covers incremental
+    behavior within a single run. Set :setting:`CONDITIONAL_REQUESTS_DIR` to
+    a directory path to persist validators on disk (one database file per
+    spider, named ``<spider name>_conditional_requests``), so that separate
+    runs, e.g. a daily cron job, can benefit from the validators collected
+    by earlier runs.
+
+    .. reqmeta:: conditional_request
+
+    To opt a single request out of all conditional-request handling, set its
+    :reqmeta:`conditional_request` meta key to ``False``. Opted-out requests
+    never get conditional headers, ``304`` responses to them are passed to
+    the spider untouched, and their responses never update the stored
+    validators.
+
+    The middleware keeps track of its activity through the following
+    :ref:`stats <topics-stats>`:
+
+    *   ``conditional_requests/conditional``: number of requests to which
+        the middleware attached conditional headers.
+
+    *   ``conditional_requests/store``: number of responses whose
+        validators were stored.
+
+    *   ``conditional_requests/not_modified``: number of ``304 Not
+        Modified`` responses that were dropped.
+
